   "source": [
    "# What drives the price of a car?\n",
    "\n",
    "![](images/kurt.jpeg)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**OVERVIEW**\n",
    "\n",
    "In this application, you will explore a dataset from Kaggle. The original dataset contained information on 3 million used cars. The provided dataset contains information on 426K cars to ensure speed of processing.  Your goal is to understand what factors make a car more or less expensive.  As a result of your analysis, you should provide clear recommendations to your client -- a used car dealership -- as to what consumers value in a used car."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### CRISP-DM Framework\n",
    "\n",
    "<center>\n",
    "    <img src = images/crisp.png width = 50%/>\n",
    "</center>\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "To frame the task, throughout our practical applications, we will refer back to a standard process in industry for data projects called CRISP-DM.  This process provides a framework for working through a data problem.  Your first step in this application will be to read through a brief overview of CRISP-DM [here](https://mo-pcco.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/BH-PCMLAI/module_11/readings_starter.zip).  After reading the overview, answer the questions below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Business Understanding\n",
    "\n",
    "From a business perspective, we are tasked with identifying key drivers for used car prices.  In the CRISP-DM overview, we are asked to convert this business framing to a data problem definition.  Using a few sentences, reframe the task as a data task with the appropriate technical vocabulary. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data Tasks\n",
    "The business task can be reframed as the following data tasks:\n",
    "- Is there one factor e.g., car paint color (the independent variable) that is good for predicting the used car prices (the dependent, outcome variable)?\n",
    "- Which factors or variables (color, condition, odometer reading, etc.) are the best predictors of the outcome variable (car price)?\n",
    "- How much do these independent variable(s) influence the outcome variable (car price)? Can we use an algorithm to model the data to determine the magnitude and sign of such influences?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Success Criteria\n",
    "- Independent variable(s) identified for predicting used car prices \n",
    "- A machine learning model successfully selected to fit the data to determine the madnitude and sign of independent variables' influence. (based on objective criteria such as model scores/metrics)\n",
    "- Business receommendations to car dearlership based on the data analysis and selected machine learning model"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Assumptions\n",
    "- As per Kaggle website, the original data (3 million used cars) was obtained in 2021. And by looking at the data, the \"max\" or highest year value is 2022. Therefore I will use year \"2022\" instead of \"2026\" as the \"base year\" for calculation of the age of the used cars. (Note: It is common for dealerships to start selling 2022 car models in late 2021.)\n",
    "- The 426,880 used cars' data is a good representation of the original 3 million used cars data, otherwise the data analysis and results may not represent the larger used car population, therefore less useful in price prediction.\n",
    "- Although \"year\" data is there, this exercise will *not* treat the dataset as a time-series. Instead, \"year\" data will be used to derive the \"age\" of the used car, so that \"age\" data can be modeled as a potential predictor of car prices.\n",
    "- ID and VIN are unique indentifiers only, not independent \"features\" for predicting car prices\n",
    "- \"condition\" data contain ordinal values in a certain ranked order (see Data Preparation section)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data Understanding\n",
    "\n",
    "After considering the business understanding, we want to get familiar with our data.  Write down some steps that you would take to get to know the dataset and identify any quality issues within.  Take time to get to know the dataset and explore what information it contains and how this could be used to inform your business understanding."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Steps to understand the data\n",
    "\n",
    "Exploratory Data Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "- Examine data using summary statistics\n",
    "- Detect skewness, and outliers\n",
    "- Identify data quality issues: missing values, duplicate rows, incorrect data types, inconsistent strings, etc.\n",
    "- Use visual charts to and correlation checks to understand variable relationships"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
