   "source": [
    "### Evaluation Criteria - Best Model Selection\n",
    "\n",
    "We will use the least Mean Squared Error (MSE) to evaluate the model performances. The smaller the MSE, the better the model fits our data.\n",
    "Judging by the Test MSE's shown in the result table and bar plot above, the following 2 models perform best:\n",
    "- Linear Regression model fitting all features (apply OneHotEncoder to categorical features)\n",
    "- Linear Regression model fitting all features with best polynomials (best degree = 1)\n",
    "\n",
    "Since the \"best degree\" = 1 for the best polynomials model, that practically means that best polynimial Linear Regression model is the same as the first straight-forward Linear Regression model fitting all features\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore we will apply the simple, straight-forward Linear Regression model, and do some *fine-tuning* to select the top 10 features \n",
    "\n",
    "*Why 10?* due to encoding of categorical features into binary numbers, we have a total of 80 \"features\" (instead of the original 11) after the transformation by encoding."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Fine-tuning best model to select top predictive features for business\n",
    "\n",
    "We will use the Linear Regression model fitting all features (apply OneHotEncoder to categorical features) again, but this time we will use a Sequential Feature Selector to select top 10 predictive features."
